It seems like ever since I increased some networking-related buffer=20
sizes, my system, an AlphaPC 164 with 256MB RAM running 7.0_RC2,=20
frequently crashes when a Mac on the network starts a Time Machine=20
backup to the Alpha (which is running netatalk/afpd). The sysctls I=20
changed are:

kern.sbmax=3D4194304
net.inet.tcp.recvbuf_max=3D4194304
net.inet.tcp.sendbuf_max=3D4194304
net.inet.tcp.recvbuf_inc=3D131072
net.inet.tcp.sendbuf_inc=3D131072

and I recompiled the kernel for:
kern.mbuf.nmbclusters =3D 16384

The alpha crashes with the following messages:

CPU 0: fatal kernel trap:

CPU 0    trap entry =3D 0x2 (memory management fault)
CPU 0    a0         =3D 0x78
CPU 0    a1         =3D 0x1
CPU 0    a2         =3D 0x0
CPU 0    pc         =3D 0xfffffc00004c7d00
CPU 0    ra         =3D 0xfffffc00004c7cc0
CPU 0    pv         =3D 0xfffffc000044da70
CPU 0    curlwp     =3D 0xfffffc000fe55980
CPU 0        pid =3D 0, comm =3D system

panic: trap
cpu0: Begin traceback...
alpha trace requires known PC =3Deject=3D
cpu0: End traceback...

So, I wanted to try to figure out what was going on, but I can't get=20
gdb to give me a stack trace:

# gdb netbsd.gdb
GNU gdb (GDB) 7.7.1
[ ... ]
Reading symbols from netbsd.gdb...done.
(gdb) target kvm /var/crash/netbsd.57.core
PC not available
<unavailable> in ?? ()
(gdb) bt
#-1 <unavailable> in ?? ()
#0  <unavailable> in ?? ()
/usr/src/external/gpl3/gdb/dist/gdb/frame.c:472: internal-error: =
get_frame_id: Assertion `fi->this_id.p' failed.
A problem internal to GDB has been detected,
further debugging may prove unreliable.
Quit this debugging session? (y or n) y

/usr/src/external/gpl3/gdb/dist/gdb/frame.c:472: internal-error: =
get_frame_id: Assertion `fi->this_id.p' failed.
A problem internal to GDB has been detected,
further debugging may prove unreliable.
Create a core file of GDB? (y or n) n

Is this related to "alpha trace requires known PC" and "PC not=20
available"?

The trap message does show a pc though, and the info from that does=20
seem to be good:

(gdb) list *0xfffffc00004c7d00
0xfffffc00004c7d00 is in fxp_txintr (/usr/src/sys/dev/ic/i82557.c:1182).
1177                    txstat =3D le16toh(txd->txd_txcb.cb_status);
1178
1179                    if ((txstat & FXP_CB_STATUS_C) =3D=3D 0)
1180                            break;
1181
1182                    bus_dmamap_sync(sc->sc_dmat, txs->txs_dmamap,
1183                        0, txs->txs_dmamap->dm_mapsize,
1184                        BUS_DMASYNC_POSTWRITE);
1185                    bus_dmamap_unload(sc->sc_dmat, txs->txs_dmamap);
1186                    m_freem(txs->txs_mbuf);

So, I wanted to mention the gdb problem, and also ask if anyone had=20
tips on how to further debug this. I guess I could manually select a=20
stack frame using the "frame" command if I knew its address, but I=20
don't know where to get that. And why would bus_dmamap_sync() cause a=20
memory management fault?

